1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,560
Think about the last breakdown
that made you drop everything.

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:06,214
The call in the middle of the night.

3
00:00:06,930 --> 00:00:09,208
Crews pulled off planned work.

4
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:11,474
The line standing still.

5
00:00:12,820 --> 00:00:14,804
Now ask a harder question.

6
00:00:15,910 --> 00:00:17,105
Was it the first time?

7
00:00:17,880 --> 00:00:21,497
Most of us have fixed the
same pump, the same gearbox,

8
00:00:21,720 --> 00:00:25,666
the same conveyor more
times than we care to count.

9
00:00:26,850 --> 00:00:28,846
Every repair feels like progress.

10
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,479
It is not. It is a bill.

11
00:00:33,390 --> 00:00:34,866
And we keep paying it.

12
00:00:35,850 --> 00:00:38,424
This video is about how to stop it.

13
00:00:39,410 --> 00:00:40,721
Find the real cause,

14
00:00:41,340 --> 00:00:44,628
fix it, and prove it stays fixed.

15
00:00:45,630 --> 00:00:47,920
Root cause analysis, or RCA,

16
00:00:48,440 --> 00:00:51,064
is a structured way to
answer one question.

17
00:00:51,980 --> 00:00:53,634
Why did this really happen?

18
00:00:54,660 --> 00:00:56,092
Not what broke.

19
00:00:57,600 --> 00:00:59,475
Why was it able to break?

20
00:01:00,910 --> 00:01:03,362
We trace a failure back through the chain

21
00:01:03,412 --> 00:01:06,962
of causes until we reach
something we can change,

22
00:01:07,960 --> 00:01:09,823
so it cannot happen again.

23
00:01:11,020 --> 00:01:12,315
The last part matters.

24
00:01:13,020 --> 00:01:17,885
A good RCA ends with a change
to the plant, to a procedure,

25
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:19,754
or to the way we work.

26
00:01:20,550 --> 00:01:23,506
If nothing changes, nothing was analysed.

27
00:01:24,390 --> 00:01:25,775
It was only discussed.

28
00:01:26,850 --> 00:01:29,040
So let me say what RCA is not.

29
00:01:29,670 --> 00:01:32,064
It is not a hunt for someone to blame.

30
00:01:32,690 --> 00:01:36,405
The moment people feel blamed,
they stop sharing facts,

31
00:01:36,920 --> 00:01:38,957
and facts are all we need.

32
00:01:39,770 --> 00:01:42,342
It is not stopping at the first cause.

33
00:01:43,030 --> 00:01:45,669
The bearing failed because it overheated.

34
00:01:46,350 --> 00:01:50,510
True, but that is where
the investigation starts.

35
00:01:51,660 --> 00:01:55,096
And it is not replacing the
part and calling it fixed.

36
00:01:56,170 --> 00:01:58,256
A new bearing treats the symptom.

37
00:01:59,270 --> 00:02:03,007
The conditions that killed
the old one are still there,

38
00:02:03,460 --> 00:02:04,993
waiting for the new one.

39
00:02:05,617 --> 00:02:07,407
Why does this matter for the plant?

40
00:02:08,547 --> 00:02:11,721
Because firefighting is expensive in ways

41
00:02:11,771 --> 00:02:14,381
that never show on the budget line.

42
00:02:15,507 --> 00:02:19,873
Crews are tied up on emergency
work, so planned work slips,

43
00:02:20,347 --> 00:02:22,774
and that sets up the next failure.

44
00:02:23,387 --> 00:02:27,797
When repeat breakdowns stop,
the plant runs without interruption.

45
00:02:28,647 --> 00:02:31,643
The cost to produce or
process each unit falls,

46
00:02:32,387 --> 00:02:37,253
because the same fixed costs are
spread over more good output.

47
00:02:38,447 --> 00:02:42,603
Profit rises. And you get
there without large spending.

48
00:02:43,377 --> 00:02:44,861
No new equipment.

49
00:02:45,337 --> 00:02:46,754
No major project.

50
00:02:47,573 --> 00:02:49,633
I call this the hidden plant.

51
00:02:50,503 --> 00:02:55,293
Every site already owns
capacity it does not get to use.

52
00:02:56,133 --> 00:02:59,726
It disappears an hour
here and ten hours there,

53
00:03:00,123 --> 00:03:01,958
to failures that keep coming back.

54
00:03:02,008 --> 00:03:07,523
Add up those lost hours across
your critical equipment and

55
00:03:07,573 --> 00:03:11,279
you can find a whole extra
line's worth of output.

56
00:03:12,343 --> 00:03:14,077
It is already built.

57
00:03:14,753 --> 00:03:17,849
Already staffed. Already paid for.

58
00:03:18,613 --> 00:03:21,527
Fixing repeat failures releases it.

59
00:03:22,333 --> 00:03:26,910
That is capacity without large
capital spending, or CAPEX,

60
00:03:27,283 --> 00:03:30,790
which simply means money
spent on new assets.

61
00:03:31,983 --> 00:03:34,933
So what does an effective RCA look like?

62
00:03:35,563 --> 00:03:37,246
It has six parts.

63
00:03:38,253 --> 00:03:41,529
One, a clear problem statement,

64
00:03:42,143 --> 00:03:43,816
with the loss in numbers.

65
00:03:45,083 --> 00:03:48,139
It can be hours, tonnes, dollars.

66
00:03:49,243 --> 00:03:53,009
If you cannot size the loss,
you cannot judge the fix.

67
00:03:53,873 --> 00:03:56,499
Two, evidence before opinions.

68
00:03:57,553 --> 00:04:01,066
Photos of failed parts, trend data,

69
00:04:01,913 --> 00:04:03,333
maintenance history, operating logs.

70
00:04:03,383 --> 00:04:07,583
Collect it before anything is

71
00:04:07,633 --> 00:04:09,599
cleaned up or thrown away.

72
00:04:10,333 --> 00:04:13,160
Three, a cause and effect tree.

73
00:04:14,043 --> 00:04:17,147
Start with the problem
and keep asking why.

74
00:04:18,223 --> 00:04:21,256
In branches, not one straight line.

75
00:04:22,317 --> 00:04:24,647
The tree goes down through three layers.

76
00:04:25,937 --> 00:04:29,521
Physical causes,
the part or the condition.

77
00:04:30,407 --> 00:04:33,846
Human causes, the decisions and actions.

78
00:04:35,167 --> 00:04:36,684
And system causes,

79
00:04:37,317 --> 00:04:39,751
also called latent causes.

80
00:04:41,047 --> 00:04:43,522
Those are hidden weaknesses in procedures,

81
00:04:44,047 --> 00:04:48,712
standards and management that
let the human error happen.

82
00:04:49,477 --> 00:04:52,832
Four, check every cause against evidence.

83
00:04:53,817 --> 00:04:57,292
If you cannot verify it,
it stays a theory.

84
00:04:58,117 --> 00:05:01,645
Five, actions with an owner and a date.

85
00:05:02,457 --> 00:05:07,553
Six, a check afterwards that the
failure really stopped coming back.

86
00:05:08,287 --> 00:05:12,083
Now let me show you how this
works on a typical problem.

87
00:05:12,950 --> 00:05:15,800
This worked example uses
illustrative numbers.

88
00:05:16,430 --> 00:05:18,395
I made them up to show the method,

89
00:05:18,590 --> 00:05:21,415
so please do not treat them as benchmarks.

90
00:05:22,970 --> 00:05:25,465
Picture a slurry pump
feeding a process line.

91
00:05:26,390 --> 00:05:27,609
No standby pump.

92
00:05:28,130 --> 00:05:31,777
No buffer tank. When it
stops, the line stops.

93
00:05:33,200 --> 00:05:36,223
In twelve months it has failed six times.

94
00:05:37,150 --> 00:05:39,409
Bearings and mechanical seals.

95
00:05:40,950 --> 00:05:44,240
A mechanical seal is the
device that keeps the slurry

96
00:05:44,290 --> 00:05:48,046
inside the pump where the shaft
passes through the casing.

97
00:05:49,060 --> 00:05:52,651
Each failure stops the
line for fourteen hours.

98
00:05:53,683 --> 00:05:55,293
Now put the loss in numbers.

99
00:05:56,773 --> 00:06:00,945
The line runs at one hundred
and twenty tonnes an hour.

100
00:06:01,733 --> 00:06:07,263
Contribution margin, the selling price
minus the variable cost of each tonne,

101
00:06:07,483 --> 00:06:08,789
is eighteen dollars.

102
00:06:09,903 --> 00:06:12,323
Fourteen hours times one hundred and

103
00:06:12,373 --> 00:06:15,958
twenty is sixteen hundred
and eighty tonnes.

104
00:06:17,303 --> 00:06:21,081
Times eighteen dollars is thirty thousand,

105
00:06:21,643 --> 00:06:23,167
two hundred and forty dollars.

106
00:06:23,217 --> 00:06:26,430
Add nine thousand,

107
00:06:26,663 --> 00:06:29,050
five hundred dollars of repair cost.

108
00:06:30,243 --> 00:06:32,420
That is thirty-nine thousand,

109
00:06:33,123 --> 00:06:35,841
seven hundred and forty dollars a failure.

110
00:06:36,963 --> 00:06:41,660
Six failures is two hundred
and thirty-eight thousand,

111
00:06:42,303 --> 00:06:44,665
four hundred and forty dollars a year.

112
00:06:45,583 --> 00:06:48,199
Next, evidence, before opinions.

113
00:06:49,013 --> 00:06:51,166
We keep the failed parts.

114
00:06:51,783 --> 00:06:55,409
The seal faces are
scored and heat cracked,

115
00:06:56,183 --> 00:06:58,561
which tells us they ran dry.

116
00:06:59,593 --> 00:07:03,189
The bearing races show wear
tracks that are off centre,

117
00:07:03,843 --> 00:07:05,801
which points to misalignment.

118
00:07:06,983 --> 00:07:09,966
Oil samples show water and solids.

119
00:07:10,913 --> 00:07:13,743
The control system shows flow at about

120
00:07:13,793 --> 00:07:17,172
sixty percent of the pump's
best efficiency flow.

121
00:07:17,933 --> 00:07:20,981
That is the point where
the pump runs smoothest.

122
00:07:21,833 --> 00:07:27,037
And the maintenance system
shows no pump alignment records

123
00:07:27,943 --> 00:07:29,684
for four of the six rebuilds.

124
00:07:30,907 --> 00:07:34,319
Now we build the tree, level by level.

125
00:07:35,467 --> 00:07:38,048
Why did the seals and bearings fail early?

126
00:07:39,217 --> 00:07:40,753
Physical causes first.

127
00:07:41,477 --> 00:07:44,894
One, the pump runs far
from its best point,

128
00:07:45,117 --> 00:07:48,054
so the shaft is pushed
sideways every turn.

129
00:07:49,407 --> 00:07:54,555
Two, poor alignment after rebuilds,
including a soft foot.

130
00:07:55,547 --> 00:07:58,933
That means one foot of
the pump base is not flat,

131
00:07:59,557 --> 00:08:02,520
so tightening the bolts bends the frame.

132
00:08:03,817 --> 00:08:06,295
Three, contaminated bearing oil.

133
00:08:07,237 --> 00:08:09,701
Four, a restricted seal flush,

134
00:08:10,957 --> 00:08:14,333
the clean liquid that cools
and protects the seal faces.

135
00:08:15,667 --> 00:08:17,258
Then the human level.

136
00:08:18,347 --> 00:08:20,544
Operators throttle the discharge valve.

137
00:08:21,447 --> 00:08:24,383
Fitters align by straight edge.

138
00:08:25,497 --> 00:08:27,581
Hoses wash down onto the bearings.

139
00:08:28,827 --> 00:08:31,030
Nobody checks the flush strainer.

140
00:08:31,950 --> 00:08:33,520
Now ask why again.

141
00:08:33,910 --> 00:08:35,666
This is the system level,

142
00:08:36,480 --> 00:08:38,454
and this is where the roots are.

143
00:08:39,290 --> 00:08:43,127
Nobody reviewed the pump
duty when throughput changed.

144
00:08:44,200 --> 00:08:46,634
There is no precision alignment standard.

145
00:08:48,030 --> 00:08:50,243
The rebuild procedure has gaps.

146
00:08:51,350 --> 00:08:53,713
There is no verification after repair.

147
00:08:53,870 --> 00:08:57,653
Flush and oil condition
are on no inspection route.

148
00:08:58,800 --> 00:09:02,164
And failures are logged
only as bearing failed,

149
00:09:02,730 --> 00:09:04,685
so the pattern stays hidden.

150
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:09,816
Fix these, and people are
no longer set up to fail.

151
00:09:10,683 --> 00:09:14,393
Before we act, we test each
cause against the evidence.

152
00:09:15,693 --> 00:09:17,235
Off its best point?

153
00:09:17,743 --> 00:09:19,361
The flow trend shows it.

154
00:09:20,323 --> 00:09:22,778
Poor alignment? The records are missing.

155
00:09:23,633 --> 00:09:27,260
A cause the evidence cannot
support gets dropped.

156
00:09:28,413 --> 00:09:32,948
Then the actions. Slow the pump
down on its variable speed drive,

157
00:09:33,723 --> 00:09:38,420
so it runs near its best point
with the valve wide open.

158
00:09:39,103 --> 00:09:40,476
That one costs nothing.

159
00:09:41,253 --> 00:09:45,759
Fit a flush flow indicator and
add it to the inspection route.

160
00:09:46,573 --> 00:09:48,102
Set an alignment standard,

161
00:09:48,653 --> 00:09:51,225
buy a laser tool and train the fitters.

162
00:09:52,503 --> 00:09:57,021
Fit bearing isolators and
breathers to the bearing housings,

163
00:09:57,523 --> 00:10:00,732
to keep wash-down water
and solids out of the oil.

164
00:10:01,463 --> 00:10:03,089
And add a rebuild checklist,

165
00:10:03,843 --> 00:10:06,257
with a vibration reading before handover.

166
00:10:07,193 --> 00:10:09,757
Each has an owner and a date.

167
00:10:11,123 --> 00:10:14,308
Total cost, thirty-two thousand dollars.

168
00:10:15,383 --> 00:10:18,713
Twelve months later, the pump
has not had a single breakdown.

169
00:10:19,223 --> 00:10:23,939
Not one. The yearly loss of two
hundred and thirty-eight thousand,

170
00:10:24,449 --> 00:10:26,559
four hundred and forty dollars is gone.

171
00:10:27,303 --> 00:10:33,017
Thirty-two thousand divided by that
saving is about an eighth of a year.

172
00:10:34,043 --> 00:10:35,976
Payback is about seven weeks.

173
00:10:36,873 --> 00:10:40,274
And eighty-four hours
of production came back.

174
00:10:41,473 --> 00:10:45,457
That is ten thousand and
eighty tonnes of capacity,

175
00:10:46,183 --> 00:10:48,553
from a pump we already owned.

176
00:10:49,923 --> 00:10:52,178
Now, bearings and seals still wear.

177
00:10:52,923 --> 00:10:54,898
That is natural deterioration,

178
00:10:55,953 --> 00:10:58,124
and no analysis removes it.

179
00:10:59,013 --> 00:11:02,433
What we removed was forced deterioration,

180
00:11:03,793 --> 00:11:06,027
the wear we were causing ourselves.

181
00:11:07,263 --> 00:11:10,718
The natural wear is now
watched with vibration,

182
00:11:11,673 --> 00:11:16,398
oil and flush checks, and the parts
are changed in a planned shutdown,

183
00:11:16,653 --> 00:11:17,653
before they fail.

184
00:11:18,350 --> 00:11:20,620
Here is what I want you to take from this.

185
00:11:21,890 --> 00:11:25,607
RCA is not an event after a big failure.

186
00:11:26,380 --> 00:11:27,771
It is a system.

187
00:11:28,550 --> 00:11:29,925
A repeatable routine.

188
00:11:31,060 --> 00:11:32,363
Set clear triggers,

189
00:11:33,140 --> 00:11:35,993
like repeat failures or hours lost.

190
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,042
Use the same steps every time.

191
00:11:40,120 --> 00:11:41,977
Track every action to closure.

192
00:11:43,170 --> 00:11:46,999
And check, at ninety days
again and twelve months,

193
00:11:47,049 --> 00:11:48,979
that the failure has gone.

194
00:11:50,210 --> 00:11:53,525
Do that consistently
and firefighting fades.

195
00:11:54,820 --> 00:11:56,494
The plant gets quieter,

196
00:11:57,090 --> 00:11:58,907
and the hidden plant appears.

197
00:11:59,890 --> 00:12:02,004
The hard part is doing it on paper.

198
00:12:02,940 --> 00:12:04,524
Trees, evidence

199
00:12:05,870 --> 00:12:09,635
and follow-up get lost in
spreadsheets and email.

200
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:15,992
So I am building a tool to make this
easy, and I will share more soon.

201
00:12:16,960 --> 00:12:17,960
Until then,

202
00:12:18,480 --> 00:12:23,054
pick one repeat failure at your
site and run the six steps.

203
00:12:24,060 --> 00:12:25,218
Thank you for watching.
